        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "For the development of optical lattice atomic clocks."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B006",
    "B001"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "Optical lattice clocks are a major advance in precision measurement, with consequences for timekeeping and tests of fundamental physics. Katori and Ye made independent, complementary contributions, and decades of refinement and repeated demonstrations give confidence in the method's maturity.",
    "case_against": "The boundary between the original clock concepts and the broader history of optical frequency metrology needs careful definition. Continued improvements also make it important to distinguish the foundational achievement from later performance milestones.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that Katori and Ye made central, complementary contributions and that the clocks' reproducibility and sustained use support recognition, while noting that the original concept and later advances are difficult to separate.",
        "response": "I agree. Repeated demonstrations and continued refinement support the maturity case, but the citation should stay focused on the clock achievement and its foundational contributions rather than imply that every later improvement belongs to the same discovery."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "pearce-mark",
        "point": "Pearce sees the clocks as a route to fundamental tests and applications such as geodesy, while asking whether their scientific impact extends beyond technical performance.",
        "response": "That is the right test. Their value is not precision for its own sake: the clocks enable new fundamental tests as well as practical timekeeping. The case should rest on that demonstrated scientific reach, not on accuracy figures alone."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The historical boundary between the original optical lattice clock concepts and the broader development of optical frequency metrology needs review."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "I serve on the executive management team of WACQT, which overlaps institutionally with Göran Johansson's Chalmers affiliation. This is a potential institutional connection; no connection to the credited B003 work is apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
